{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","record":{"id":"scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","kind":"claim","label":"Finite adaptive Gaussian offsets are bounded by the SK optimum","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r6-source-finite-adaptive-lower","locator":"QuadraticSK.lean:74–88; OAI.SKValueG.finite_adaptive_SK_lower","statement":"For n>0, a finite number K of measurable zero-or-unit directions depending on preceding Gaussian columns, and a selected spin with measurable adaptive offset, E[adaptiveOffset]/(n sqrt(n)) is at most groundStateSequence(n).","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower_lean_source"],"boundary":"Source-inspected statement with quantified guards retained. Authored proof correspondence, not elaborated dependency extraction or a fresh formal acceptance."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-adaptive-lower","anchor":"gaussian-adaptive-lower","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-adaptive-lower","anchor":"gaussian-adaptive-lower","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"adaptive-gaussian-lower\"></a>\n\n#### 10.2.2 Adaptive directions and a finite-spin inequality\n\nChoose each zero-or-unit direction measurably from columns already\nrevealed. Conditional on that history, the current residual candidate\nvectors and offsets are fixed, so the preceding recursion applies.\nMore precisely, conditional on the revealed history, the expected next\ncontinuation value, including its accumulated offsets, equals the current\none. Iteration therefore preserves the initial expected maximum. For any measurable\nchoice of a candidate from the final history, its accumulated offset is\nbounded by the remaining conditional expected maximum. Hence\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\,d_{s(\\mathrm{history})}^{\\rm final}\n\\le\\mathcal V(w,0).\n$$\nContraction of each residual, the finite candidate set and Gaussian\ncolumn moments justify the expectations. Adaptivity does not turn a\nfuture column into part of the conditioning history.\n[Scalar implementation, adaptive recursion][scalar-adaptive].\n\nTake all spins $w_s\\in\\{-1,1\\}^n$ as candidates. The full quadratic\nmatrix has off-diagonal coefficients $(G_{ij}+G_{ji})/\\sqrt2$, which\nare independent standard normals, and a centered diagonal contribution\ncommon to every spin. Thus\n$$\n\\mathcal V(\\mathrm{spins},0)=\\sqrt n\\,\\mathbb E\\max_\\sigma H_n(\\sigma).\n$$\nDividing the offset inequality by $n\\sqrt n$ gives a bound by $e_n$.\nThe implementation proves this normalization through equality of Gaussian\nincrement distances in [the finite adaptive SK comparison][scalar-finite-adaptive].\n\nTo obtain the desired coefficients, take independent rows\n$z^{(1)},z^{(2)},\\ldots$, each with the law of $(z_0,\\ldots,z_K)$,\nand put\n$$\n\\widehat g_n=n^{-1/2}(g(z^{(1)}),\\ldots,g(z^{(n)})).\n$$\nLet $v_{n,j}$ be the normalized Gram–Schmidt vector obtained from\n$\\widehat f_{0,n},\\ldots,\\widehat f_{j,n}$; a zero remainder is\nnormalized to zero. Start the adaptive procedure with a zero direction\nto reveal column $z_0$. After seeing columns through $z_j$,\n$v_{n,j}$ is available, and the next column is $z_{j+1}$.\nThis explains both the extra column and the shift.\n\nLet $R_{n,j}$ project away $v_{n,0},\\ldots,v_{n,j-1}$ and set\n$$\nE_{n,j}=\\langle C_{v_{n,j}}(R_{n,j}\\widehat S_n),\n                       \\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\rangle.\n$$\nThe raw spin and fresh column are each $\\sqrt n$ times their empirical\nversions; $C_v$ is quadratic in its input. Consequently their revealed\nenergy is exactly $n\\sqrt n E_{n,j}$. Transposing the array between\nindependent rows and independent columns preserves its Gaussian law.\nThe finite comparison therefore gives, for every $n>0$,\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{j<K}E_{n,j}\\le e_n.\n$$\nThis is an actual choice of the adaptive directions and final rowwise\nspin, not an uninstantiated conditional inequality.\n[Empirical directions and normalization][scalar-empirical-directions];\n[row–column transposition and finite bound][scalar-transpose].\n","span":{"start_byte":185263,"end_byte":188163,"start_line":3960,"end_line":4022},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-adaptive-lower","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-adaptive-lower"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_gaussian_column_split%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_gaussian_column_split","kind":"supports","semantic_class":"curated_argument_support","derived":false,"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_gaussian_column_split%22%2C%22supports%22%5D"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.